{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>To all flesh of his own kind, you shall not approach to uncover nakedness</b>. The reason for the prohibition of forbidden relations among one's own flesh is not explicitly stated. The Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:49) that it is to diminish intercourse and to make it repugnant, and to content oneself with it sparingly. And these women whom Scripture forbade among a wife's kin are those who are constantly present with him, and likewise those among his own kin are present with him and he is secluded with them. And the Rav says this same reason for all of them. And Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra already wrote (on Leviticus 18:6) as well that because the inclination of man's heart is like that of beasts, it is impossible to prohibit all females, and so He prohibited all those who are found with him at all times. But this reason is very weak, for it would mean that Scripture imposes karet for these because they happen to be with him sometimes, yet permits a man to marry women by the hundreds and thousands — and what harm would there be if he married his daughter alone, as is permitted to the children of Noah (Sanhedrin 58a), or married two sisters as did our father Jacob? And a man would have no more fitting marriages than if he were to marry his daughter to his son who is older than her, and bequeath them his inheritance, and they would be fruitful and multiply in his house, for He did not create the earth to be a waste; He formed it to be inhabited. And we have nothing transmitted on this matter, but according to reason there is in this matter a secret from the secrets of formation cleaving to the soul, and it is part of the all-inclusive secret of conception to which we have already alluded. And know that intercourse is a thing far removed and repugnant in the Torah except for the perpetuation of the species, and that from which no offspring can be born is forbidden, and likewise that which is not good for existence and will not succeed — the Torah will prohibit it. This is the reason for \"to all flesh of his own kind\" (here) and \"for he has exposed his own flesh\" (Leviticus 20:19): the matter is to be kept far from one's kin, and the expression is derived from \"those who remain in Zion and those who are left in Jerusalem\" (Isaiah 4:3), and therefore it says \"they are your flesh's own — it is wickedness\" (Leviticus 18:17), meaning they are not fit for marriage and union for they would not succeed, but it is wickedness, merely the plotting of desire alone. And behold, the forbidden relations are among the ḥukkim — matters that are a royal decree, and the decree is the matter that arises in the mind of the King, who is wise in the governance of His kingdom, and who knows the need and the benefit in that commandment that He commands through it, and He does not disclose it to the people except to the wise men who are His counselors."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The Rav refers to Maimonides. The passage from the Guide for the Perplexed is book III, chapter 49."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:20.581993168Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}